      "id": 1,
      "text": "Madison was incorporated on December 12, 1809, and was named after President James Madison.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town appears on Georgia’s Antebellum Trail and is one of the state’s Historic Heartland cities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Madison’s historic Eagle Tavern, built in the 1800s, still welcomes visitors to taste local heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://legacylorega.org/where-to-visit/madison-ga/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Madison‑Morgan Cultural Center showcases regional performing and visual arts, plus a permanent exhibit on Georgia’s Piedmont region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Madison%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s historic downtown displays a mix of Greek Revival, Beaux‑Arts, and other architectural styles that draw history buffs.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
